.TH CJSON 7 "2026-10-06" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Overview"
.SH NAME
cjson \- overview of the JSON parser, serializer and DOM
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
cjson parses JSON text (RFC 8259) into a tree of nodes.  A program can
read and change that tree.  cjson then writes the tree back as JSON.
An opaque cjson handle identifies each node.
.fi
.SH "NODE TYPES"
.nf
cjson_type(3) gives the type of a node, as a cjson_node_type_t:

  CJSON_NULL         null
  CJSON_BOOL         true or false, read with cjson_bool_val(3)
  CJSON_INTEGER      a number with no '.' and no exponent that fits a
                     long long, read with cjson_int_val(3)
  CJSON_FLOAT        all other numbers, a double, read with
                     cjson_double_val(3)
  CJSON_STRING       a UTF-8 string, read with cjson_str_val(3)
  CJSON_LIST         an array of child nodes
  CJSON_DICTIONARY   an object: string keys, each with a child node

A value accessor stops the program through ccol_fatal_err() when the
node has a different type.  This includes a NULL node.  Therefore,
call cjson_type(3) before you read a node whose type the input sets.
cjson_parse(3) gives the rules for large integers, strings and UTF-8.
.fi
.SH "PATHS"
.nf
cjson_get(3), cjson_set(3) and cjson_delete(3) find a node through a
string of components with dots between them, for example
"users.#0.name".  On an array, a component "#N" is the element at
index N.  All other components are object keys.  Write "\e." for a dot
in a key, and "\e\e" for a backslash.  cjson_set(3) creates a missing
object key at the leaf.  It never creates a missing parent, and never
an array element after the end.
.fi
.SH "ORDER"
.nf
An object keeps its members in insertion order:
  - For a parsed document, the order of the text.
  - For a tree that a program makes, the order of the calls that added
    the keys.
A replacement keeps the position of its key.  The serializers,
cjson_clone(3) and the cursor of cjson_dictionary_first(3) all use
this order.  Therefore, the output for one tree is the same in each
run.
.fi
.SH "OWNERSHIP"
.nf
cjson_parse(3), cjson_clone(3) and the cjson_create_*(3) functions give
a tree that the caller owns.  Free that tree with cjson_destroy(3).

cjson_get(3), cjson_list_get(3), cjson_dictionary_get(3) and the
dictionary cursor give borrowed references.  The tree continues to own
them.  To attach such a node at a different location, clone it first.

cjson_list_push(3) and cjson_dictionary_set(3) take the child.  Only
ccol_invalid_args leaves the child with the caller.

Each node keeps a record of its allocator.  Therefore, the destroy of
a tree needs no allocator from the caller.  The allocator of the root
frees a string from cjson_serialize(3).
.fi
.SH "LIMITS"
.nf
A parse refuses nesting deeper than 500 containers.  The serializers
and cjson_clone(3) also refuse it for a tree that a program makes.
The size of the tree of a parse is linear in the length of the input;
cjson_parse(3) gives the limit.  A string never holds a null byte,
because each string is a null-terminated C string.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
A tree is not thread safe.  Many threads can read one tree at the
same time while no thread changes it.  Separate trees are independent.
Separate threads can parse and serialize at the same time.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cjson_parse (3),
.BR cjson_serialize (3),
.BR cjson_get (3),
.BR cjson_set (3),
.BR cjson_dictionary_first (3),
.BR cjson_list_push (3),
.BR cjson_destroy (3),
